WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:02.610
(staticky chattering)

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.400
- [Narrator] It's July 20th, 1969.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.620
Across the country, an
estimated 650 million Americans

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.923
have tuned in to watch the
first men land on the moon.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.540
Also watching is Jo Thompson.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:17.840
You don't know her name yet

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.190
but without Jo and her
sister seamstresses,

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.385
those men would've never made
it to the moon and back again.

00:00:23.385 --> 00:00:26.334
- [Neil Armstrong] That's
one small step for man,

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:28.584
one giant leap for mankind.

00:00:34.355 --> 00:00:36.961
(playful string quartet music)

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:39.211
(whirring)

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.390
- My name is Jo Thompson
and back in the '60s,

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.810
I sewed on spacesuits for Neil Armstrong,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.480
Buzz Aldrin, and Michael Collins.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.920
And we would bury the thread like that.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.610
And see, now it's tucked under.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.290
When I was about 6 or 7

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.660
was when my aunt taught me about sewing

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.543
so that I was able to make
a pretty decent doll dress.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.730
I am proud I was a seamstress,

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:12.060
and I'm glad that what we
did was a great success;

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.340
but the seamstresses have
really gone undocumented.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.120
Right there, that's me.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.610
Without our attention to
detail and our dedication,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.903
they would not have been
able to do what they did.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:28.750
- [Narrator] In 1966,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.590
Jo was recruited onto an
elite team of seamstresses

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.660
by former bra factory ILC Dover.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.810
In the years to come,

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.370
she'd help create some of the
most complicated technology

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.140
of the 20th century.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.080
There were layers of insulation.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.970
The cemented area that held
the air when they inflated it,

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.720
that was the inner layer.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.970
Then there was an outer layer.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:54.630
Inside even all of that,
he had like a spandex suit

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.040
that had tubes running in through it.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.623
Then there was that neck ring
that the helmet attached to.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:06.503
It was quite an engineering
marvel. (laughing)

00:02:09.948 --> 00:02:12.800
(quiet somber music)

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.710
My job required me to do things that were

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.910
technical and challenging.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.330
When we cut the items out,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.250
it had to be placed back
on a pattern to be accepted

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:30.250
and it was not more
than 1/64th of an inch.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.230
The tolerance of 1/64th
of an inch is very small.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.163
It's probably not much
more than a hair breadth.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.380
If you made a needle hole by
stitching wrong somewhere,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.410
when the suit was under pressure,
then the suit would leak.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.430
If the suit started leaking air,

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:50.840
there's only one thing could happen

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.913
and that would be he
wouldn't make it back home.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.490
Fifty years ago is a long time.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:06.010
I think it was fairly
early in the evening.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.560
It was when he got out
and stepped on the moon

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.700
that I really felt like
this is the final test

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.051
of what we did.

00:03:14.051 --> 00:03:15.630
- [Astronaut] Things are
looking great here, Joe.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.340
- [Jo] I had a small black and white TV

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.283
with not a whole lot of good reception.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.530
I suppose there was more people there

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.440
but to me, I was the only one there,

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.720
and I was the only one
that was responsible

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.410
for this man being on the moon,

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:36.020
holding my breath to make
sure he made it back again.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.530
Every time I see the moon,

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.740
it reminds me of all
the people I worked with

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.360
and what we made, and I
look up there and I think,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.030
"A man was actually on that moon

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.670
"and he actually wore something
that I had my hand on."

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.700
Can't believe it.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.617
- [Armstrong] The eagle has landed.

